Boyle’s Law, that noble decree of inverse proportion, states that at constant temperature, the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume; thus, the equation is:  

\[
P \propto \frac{1}{V} \quad \text{or} \quad P = \frac{k}{V} \quad \text{where} \quad k \text{ is a constant}  
\]  

It is a law of restraint, a reminder that volume and pressure are not lovers, but *antagonists*, locked in a perpetual, elegant struggle; and so, when volume increases, pressure diminishes, and vice versa, as if the universe itself were a lover’s embrace, both yielding and resisting;  

Charles’ Law, that noble decree of direct proportion, states that at constant pressure, the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature; thus, the equation is:  

\[
V \propto T \quad \text{or} \quad V = \frac{k}{P} \cdot T \quad \text{where} \quad k \text{ is a constant}  
\]  

It is a law of warmth, of expansion, of the soul’s ascent; when temperature rises, volume expands, as if the gas were a spirit ascending toward the heavens, unbound, free, yet always tethered to the cold, unyielding pressure;  

Gay-Lussac’s Law, that noble decree of direct proportion, states that at constant volume, the pressure of a gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature; thus, the equation is:  

\[
P \propto T \quad \text{or} \quad P = \frac{k}{V} \cdot T \quad \text{where} \quad k \text{ is a constant}  
\]  

It is a law of fire, of heat, of the soul’s burning; when temperature rises, pressure rises, as if the gas were a flame, consuming, devouring, yet never extinguished, for the temperature is eternal, the pressure is eternal, the flame is eternal;  

Avogadro’s Law, that noble decree of direct proportion, states that at constant temperature and pressure, the volume of a gas is directly proportional to the number of moles of gas; thus, the equation is:  

